<html>
<title>Branching to COMX slots and Elf EMS pages</title>
<body>


<h1>Branching to COMX slots and Elf EMS pages</h1>


<h2>Cross-slot branch correction</h2>

<p>
Any long branch instruction or branch data <strong>from</strong> a slot or EMS page to a non-slot/page memory location will be corrected if affected by an insert or delete. However, branches <strong>from</strong> non-slot/page memory <strong>to</strong> a slot or EMS page area will only be corrected if:
</p>
<ul>
<li>The same slot or page code is used for both configuration ranges</li>
<li>A <tt>LBR Sxx,yyyy</tt> macro is used for branch instructions</li>
<li>A <tt>: Sxx,yyyy</tt> macro is used for branch data</li>
<li>A <tt>LDL Sxx,Rx,yyyy</tt> macro is used for loading a data location</li>
<li>A <tt>LDRL Sxx,Rx,yyyy</tt> macro is used for loading a data location in reversed byte order</li>
</ul>


<h2>LBR macro</h2>

<p>
The <tt>LBR Sxx,yyyy</tt> macro can be used for any long branch instruction (<tt>LBDF</tt>, <tt>LBNF</tt>, <tt>LBNQ</tt>, <tt>LBNZ</tt>, <tt>LBQ</tt>, <tt>LBR</tt> or <tt>LBZ</tt>). <tt>Sxx</tt> specifies the slot code or EMS page where the branch should jump to, and <tt>yyyy</tt> defines the branch address.
</p>


<h2>Example</h2>

<p>
For example, using a COMX with SB and the following <a href="DirectConfiguration.htm">Program Configurations</a> and code:
</p>

<p>
Configuration RAM:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_slots_ram_conf.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<p>
Configuration bank 8, slot code 11:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_slots_bank0.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<p>
Configuration bank 9, slot code 31:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_slots_bank1.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<p>
Code in RAM:
</p>

<p>
Inserting at address C010 (hexadecimal) in RAM bank 0, slot code 11 (hexadecimal) will result in:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_slots_ram.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the <tt>S11</tt> branch is corrected as a byte is inserted in bank 8, but the <tt>S31</tt> branch remains unchanged as no byte is inserted in bank 9.
</p>


<h2>Other macros</h2>

<p>
The <tt>: Sxx,yyyy</tt>, <tt>LDL Sxx,Rx,yyyy</tt>, and <tt>LDRL Sxx,Rx,yyyy</tt> macros work the same as the <tt>LBR Sxx,yyyy</tt> macro, except they are used for branch addresses and loading memory locations.
</p>
</body>
</html>